WEBVTT

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.450
A typical GGUF workflow starts with choosing or preparing a model file,

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:06.000
then selecting a compatible runtime.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.000
The file may come from a model repository, a trusted release page,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.000
or a conversion process.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.965
After download, the runtime loads the file, reads the metadata, allocates memory,

00:00:14.965 --> 00:00:17.000
and prepares the tokenizer.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.190
At that point, the user can test prompts through a command line, web interface, local API,

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:22.000
or notebook.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.930
Several runtime settings shape the experience.

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:28.200
Context length controls how much prompt and conversation history the model can consider.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.965
Execution may be CPU-only, GPU-only for supported layers, or hybrid,

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:35.000
where some work is offloaded to the GPU and some remains on the CPU.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.100
Thread settings, batch settings, and memory options can affect speed, responsiveness,

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.000
and stability.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.000
These are engineering choices, not just performance trivia.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.939
Testing should include more than whether the model answers once.

00:00:44.939 --> 00:00:50.000
Observe memory use, loading time, prompt processing speed, token generation speed,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.000
and output quality for the actual task.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.950
A model that works well for short chat may be poor for long document analysis.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:59.000
A quantization that is fast may lose too much quality for careful reasoning.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.175
A configuration that fits one machine may fail on another with less VRAM or RAM.

00:01:03.175 --> 00:01:05.971
Documenting the working configuration is part of the workflow.

00:01:05.971 --> 00:01:10.015
Record the model name, source, file hash if available, quantization, runtime version,

00:01:10.015 --> 00:01:14.000
context length, GPU offload setting, key parameters, and hardware used.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.245
This makes results reproducible and helps compare variants fairly.

00:01:17.245 --> 00:01:18.550
Without documentation,

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:23.220
local AI experiments quickly become a pile of model files and vague memories about what

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.000
seemed to work.
